Q: How can I get a source code of Python function which was declared by using &#39;exec&#39; inside other function? A: <p>The main problem is that <code>inspect.getsource</code> needs a filesystem reference where to read the source code of the function... but being created dynamically it's a problem.</p>
<p>The previous solution with <code>linecache.cache</code> + <code>source</code> is <code>inspect.getsource</code> compatible but causes an execution slowdown.</p>
<p>If a speedup is desired than one should <strong>drop</strong> the idea of <code>inspect.getsource</code> (its pure Python code and needs input from file-system). Two possibilities come to my mind:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Use a external <code>json</code>-file as a kind of database with entries of the form <em>func_identifier: body</em>, load it globally and use it inside the <code>declare_func</code>. This should not cause the <code>OSError: could not get source code</code> anymore.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Add the source code as function attribute</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<p>Here solution 2: how to implement a way to track the source code of a dynamically created function and make its (some!) of its attributes <em>read-only</em>.</p>
<p>! For a further speed-up move the class definition at module level !</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def declare_func():
    func_code = \
'''def new_func():
    &quot;&quot;&quot;doc: bla&quot;&quot;&quot; # &lt;------------- added doc!
    print(&quot;Hello, world!&quot;)'''

    scope = {}
    exec(func_code, None, scope)
    f = scope['new_func'] # f = list(scope.values()).pop()

    class DGFunction:
        &quot;&quot;&quot;Dynamically Generated Function. It contains the source code and its attributes are immutable.&quot;&quot;&quot;

        __slots__ = ('_func', '_source')

        def __init__(self, func, source):
            self._func = func
            self._source = source

        # make the source code of the dynamically generated function read-only
        @property
        def getsource(self): return self._source
        # error for write attempt
        @getsource.setter
        def getsource(self, value): raise AttributeError(&quot;Error, source code of dynamically generated function cannot be modified.&quot;)

        # call the function            
        def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs): return self._func(*args, **kwargs)  

        # attribute mapping: class -&gt; function
        def __repr__(self): return repr(self._func)
        def __getattr__(self, name): return getattr(self._func, name)
        @property
        def __name__(self): return self._func.__name__
        @property
        def __doc__(self): return self._func.__doc__
        @property
        def __code__(self): return self._func.__code__

    return DGFunction(f, func_code)


f = declare_func()

print(f)
print(&quot;-&gt;&quot;, f.getsource) # &lt;- this is how to get the original source code!
try:    f.getsource = &quot;xxx&quot;
except: print(&quot;Attempt to modify the source code failed&quot;)
try:    f.yyy = &quot;yyy&quot;
except: print(&quot;Attempt to add a new attribute failed&quot;)

print(f.__name__)
print(f.__doc__)
print(dir(f))
print(f.__code__.co_filename)
print(type(f))
</code></pre>
<p>Output</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;function new_func at 0x10f4f3600&gt;
-&gt; def new_func():
    &quot;&quot;&quot;doc: bla&quot;&quot;&quot;
    print(&quot;Hello, world!&quot;)
Attempt to modify the source code failed
Attempt to add a new attribute failed
new_func
doc: bla
&lt;string&gt;
&lt;class '__main__.declare_func.&lt;locals&gt;.DGFunction'&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: React input currency mask A: <p>You can use this.</p>
<pre><code>      &lt;CurrencyInput
        prefix=&quot;$&quot;
        id=&quot;input-example&quot;
        name=&quot;input-name&quot;
        decimalsLimit=&quot;2&quot;
        onValueChange={(value, name) =&gt; console.log(value, name)}
      /&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to set URL for Maven dependency A: <p>I had this problem where my lookup for dependencies were failing. As it was kind of replacing org.xxx as org/xxx and none of the dependencies were pulled.</p>

<p>The url for default Central Maven repository is <a href="http://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/</a>.
Unless you want to fetch artifacts from different location (like local artifactory etc.,)  Remove  section or you can still have </p>

<pre><code>       &lt;repository&gt; 
           &lt;id&gt;central&lt;/id&gt;
           &lt;url&gt;http://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2/&lt;/url&gt;
       &lt;/repository&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>mvn clean install
it should download all repositories that you are looking for.</p>
